<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Dance · date unknown · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Tōryanse</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">通りゃんせ</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Tōryanse</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese game song: children walk under an arch of hands and the one caught when it stops is out. Traffic lights play it too.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese children</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toryanse">Tōryanse</a>&quot; is a traditional Japanese children's song (warabe uta). There are many theories about its origin, but all agree that it portrays an exchange between an ordinary person and a guard who keeps a checkpoint; one theory places the checkpoint at Kawagoe Castle. In earlier times, when many infants died, people celebrated when a child survived to the age of seven (and also three and five, the ages kept in the festival of Shichi-Go-San), and ordinary people were allowed to visit the shrine inside the castle grounds only on such special occasions.</p><p>The song belongs to a traditional game. Two children face each other and join hands to form an arch, the &quot;checkpoint&quot;, and the others walk underneath in a line and round again in circles. The child who is under the arch when the song ends is caught, much as in the English-language game &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-bridge-is-falling-down">London Bridge Is Falling Down</a>&quot;.</p><p>The tune is now a common choice for the music played by traffic lights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> when it is safe to cross, a reminder of the game: one may cross until the music stops. It is also heard in many forms of popular culture, among them crossing scenes in anime.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A dialogue between a traveller and the guard of a checkpoint on the way to a shrine, sung as children pass one by one under an arch of hands, not knowing who will be caught when it ends.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">通りゃんせ 通りゃんせ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Tōryanse tōryanse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Go through, go through.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ここはどこの 細道じゃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">koko wa doko no hosomichi ja</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here, whose narrow path is this?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">天神さまの 細道じゃ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Tenjin-sama no hosomichi ja</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is the narrow path of Tenjin-sama.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ちっと通して 下しゃんせ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">chitto tōshite kudashanse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Please let us through a little.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">御用のないもの 通しゃせぬ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">goyō no nai mono tōshasenu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those who have no business here are not let through.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">この子の七つの お祝いに</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kono ko no nanatsu no oiwai ni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the celebration of this child's seventh year</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">お札を納めに まいります</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">ofuda o osame ni mairimasu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are going to offer up a talisman.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">行きはよいよい 帰りはこわい</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">iki wa yoi yoi kaeri wa kowai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Going is fine, fine; coming back is frightening.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">こわいながらも</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kowai nagara mo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Though it is frightening,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">通りゃんせ 通りゃんせ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tōryanse tōryanse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>go through, go through.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Japanese Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E9%80%9A%E3%82%8A%E3%82%83%E3%82%93%E3%81%9B" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>lines 1 and 10: 'tōryanse' is an old Edo-period polite imperative, 'please pass through'.</li><li>line 2: literally 'here is the narrow path of where?'</li><li>line 3: Tenjin-sama is Sugawara no Michizane worshipped as a god, at a Tenmangū shrine.</li><li>line 6: the seventh year is the age of the Shichi-Go-San shrine visit, when a child was thought safely past early childhood.</li><li>line 7: the ofuda, a shrine talisman, is given back to the shrine in thanks.</li><li>line 8: why the way back is frightening is not said in the song; explanations differ.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
